From 72729f3ef6aa73dde2e2c36664e78d6148826239 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oleg Eterevsky <oleg@eterevsky.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 22:35:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] metal: keep temporaries in the fence bookkeeping when a
 previous encoder wrote their buffer

CommandEncoder::end_encoding() erases every temporary's buffer from
all_inputs_/all_outputs_ before it emits the cross-command-encoder fence
waits, on the grounds that temporaries do not cross encoder boundaries.
That holds for the temporary ARRAY but not for its BUFFER:

* eval_gpu helpers alias a caller's buffer into a temporary --
  compute_dynamic_offset does exactly this (offset.copy_shared_buffer(
  indices)) and then registers the alias as a temporary, and
* MetalAllocator recycles freed buffers immediately, with no regard for
  command buffers still in flight.

In both cases the buffer may be one a previous command encoder wrote, and
the wait this function emits is the only thing ordering the two -- Metal
buffers are allocated hazard-tracking-untracked and the command buffers are
created with unretained references, so nothing else will. Erasing the entry
silently drops the dependency and the kernel reads stale data.

Keep the entry when prev_ce_outputs_ knows the buffer; erase it otherwise, so
the map stays small for genuinely fresh temporaries.

Measured on an M5 Max (macOS 26.5, Metal 4): a 36-layer Qwen3-8B prefill
graph returned a different answer on most evaluations and lost 5 of 15
outputs entirely; with this patch it returns the same values at 40, 512 and
2048 MB per command buffer.
---
 mlx/backend/metal/device.cpp | 25 ++++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/mlx/backend/metal/device.cpp b/mlx/backend/metal/device.cpp
index 29eecf55..479cd109 100644
--- a/mlx/backend/metal/device.cpp
+++ b/mlx/backend/metal/device.cpp
@@ -434,22 +434,33 @@ void CommandEncoder::end_encoding() {
   //   from the map to limit the growth of the map and avoid unnecessary waits
   // - Temporaries are a special case as they do not cross command encoder
   //   boundaries. These can be removed early from the encoders inputs and
-  //   outputs since they don't need synchronization.
+  //   outputs since they don't need synchronization -- unless a previous
+  //   command encoder wrote the temporary's buffer, see below.
   if (!encoder_) {
     return;
   }
 
-  // Remove temporaries from inputs and outputs.
-  for (auto& t : temporaries_) {
-    all_outputs_.erase(t.buffer().ptr());
-    all_inputs_.erase(t.buffer().ptr());
-  }
-
   // Keep references to the fences we waited on and put them in the completion
   // handler so they are not prematurely released.
   std::unordered_set<NS::SharedPtr<MTL::Fence>> waiting_on;
   {
     std::lock_guard lk(outputs_mtx_);
+    // Remove temporaries from inputs and outputs.
+    //
+    // A temporary ARRAY does not cross command encoder boundaries, but its
+    // BUFFER can: eval_gpu helpers alias a caller's buffer into a temporary
+    // (`copy_shared_buffer`), and the allocator recycles freed buffers with
+    // no regard for command buffers still in flight. When a previous encoder
+    // wrote that buffer, the wait below is the only thing ordering the two,
+    // so the temporary has to keep its place in the bookkeeping.
+    for (auto& t : temporaries_) {
+      auto ptr = t.buffer().ptr();
+      if (prev_ce_outputs_.find(ptr) != prev_ce_outputs_.end()) {
+        continue;
+      }
+      all_outputs_.erase(ptr);
+      all_inputs_.erase(ptr);
+    }
     for (auto& in : all_inputs_) {
       if (auto it = prev_ce_outputs_.find(in); it != prev_ce_outputs_.end()) {
         // If we've already waited on a fence, don't wait on it again.
